.TH SYS_TASKS 2
.SH NAME
sys_tasks \- list the processes (SYS_TASKS)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include "mk.h"

long sys_tasks(mk_task_t *t, long max, int after);

typedef struct {
    int32_t pid, uid, state, cpu;
    int32_t threads, ppid;   /* ppid 0: started by the kernel */
    uint64_t pages;          /* memory in use, 4 KiB pages */
    char name[16];
    uint64_t quota;          /* memory limit in pages (0: none) */
} mk_task_t;
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sys_tasks
fills
.I t
with up to
.I max
processes whose pid is above
.IR after ,
in pid order. To list them all, call again with the last pid received
until fewer than
.I max
come back.
.PP
A process's
.I state
is its most active thread's:
.BR TS_RUN ,
.BR TS_READY ,
.B TS_SEND
(queued on a port),
.B TS_RECV
(waiting for a message),
.B TS_REPLY
(waiting for an answer),
.B TS_LOOKUP
(waiting for a port's name),
.B TS_SLEEP
or
.B TS_WAIT
(for a child).
.I cpu
is where that thread runs, or last ran.
.I name
is the program's first word, at most 15 characters.
.PP
Anyone may call it;
.BR ps (1)
prints it.
.SH RETURN VALUE
The number of entries filled, or a negative error.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EFAULT
.I t
is not writable.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ps (1),
.BR sys_info (2),
.BR sys_ident (2)
